<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  SPFN Mobile — the Android harness's window theme.

  The twin of examples/android-compose/src/main/res/values/themes.xml, for the same reason
  and with the same one attribute. Both apps' windows are white in both appearances, so both
  need the status bar's foreground drawn dark, and neither may reach a design system to say
  it (decision C2).

  The PARENT is the theme this app already named in its manifest, unchanged:
  Theme.DeviceDefault.Light.NoActionBar. NoActionBar because a title strip would draw "SPFN
  Harness" over this screen's own readouts and over the SDK's header inside the flow it
  hosts, and it would take back the rows [RunnerBlock]'s arithmetic assumes. Light because
  this screen is drawn on `Paper` and `SpfnTokens` resolves to the same white palette in
  both appearances: a theme that went dark would paint `FlowHost.cover()` — which reads
  `android:colorBackground` off this theme — near-black behind screens that are still white.
  Declaring the parent here rather than in the manifest changes nothing about what that
  attribute resolves to, and it changes nothing about the layout either: a status bar
  FOREGROUND is a colour, not an inset, so `HarnessScreen`'s `WindowInsets.systemBars`
  padding and the 617dp-against-714dp reading in [RunnerBlock] are the numbers they were.

  The ATTRIBUTE is `android:windowLightStatusBar`. Left unset the clock and the icons follow
  the system's appearance, and on a dark-mode Galaxy Z Flip4 that put a light foreground on
  this app's white: the readouts a runner reads were unaffected, but the person watching the
  run could not see the clock.

  It is declared and not set in code. `WindowInsetsControllerCompat` would do the same job
  from the activity, at the cost of a rule that only holds once a composition has run and of
  a second place the appearance is decided. The window is the app's, and this is the app
  saying what its window is.

  `android:windowLightNavigationBar` is deliberately ABSENT. It is API 27 and this
  repository's minSdk is 24, so it would need a values-v27 split or a lint suppression to
  say something that is already true: a three-button navigation bar is drawn on the system's
  own grey with dark icons, and the gesture pill takes its colour from the content behind
  it. There is nothing here for it to fix.
-->
<resources>

    <style name="Theme.SpfnHarness" parent="@android:style/Theme.DeviceDefault.Light.NoActionBar">
        <item name="android:windowLightStatusBar">true</item>
    </style>

</resources>
